WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.620
Paul Baumann: The report in front of you sets out, as always, the latest financial performance

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:21.440
data for the commissioning system, on this
occasion, up to the end of the third quarter,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.060
and indeed, our forecast for the financial
year which ends in a slightly worrying seven

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:30.280
weeks’ time. As we have reflected before,
this was a year of considerable challenges

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:36.039
for the NHS as a whole, with CCGs in particular
facing an uphill struggle to make ends meet

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:40.550
while creating the lion’s share of the £800
million system reserve we needed to set aside

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.950
at the beginning of the year to cover risks
across the commissioning and provider sectors.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.750
Board members will of course recall that there
was no separate funding for this in the Spending

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:54.710
Review, so they have to stretch their efficiency
programmes to create the required headroom

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:55.710
for that reserve.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.520
In that context, I am pleased to be able to
report that the year-to-date position has

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:06.920
improved to within 0.1% of budget overall,
but the core forecast for the commissioning

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.110
sector as a whole before the positive impact
of releasing the £800 million reserve remains

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:17.030
at an effective break-even, and the risks
to the delivery of that break-even position

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:21.401
have reduced steadily from over £0.5 billion
at quarter one to nearly £300 million at

00:01:21.401 --> 00:01:26.210
the half-year point, and £148 million in
the quarter-three report you have in front

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:31.560
of you. We do still have a way to go if we
are going to identify sufficient mitigations

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:37.220
to be confident about meeting our objective,
but we are heading in the right direction.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.540
Appendix two shows you some more detail on
the broadly balanced core forecast position

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.970
I have just indicated. Despite a £538 million
increase in their delivery of efficiencies

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:55.259
compared to last year, CCGs are showing year-to-date
overspends averaging 0.8% and affecting 98

00:01:55.259 --> 00:02:00.880
CCGs, a similar number to the report I gave
you in month seven. Some of these organisations

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.840
are still expected to recover their positions
in the final months of the year as a result

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.039
of the turnaround actions they have been taking,
with support from the local NHS England teams,

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:14.999
but we do still anticipate that at least 70
CCGs, a third of the sector, will finish the

00:02:14.999 --> 00:02:18.120
year with overspends.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.989
You can see that specialised commissioning
is expecting to balance its books by the end

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:27.249
of the year, despite significant ongoing challenges,
particularly in the South region, and I think

00:02:27.249 --> 00:02:31.700
we just need to acknowledge the significant
turnaround that has taken place in the specialised

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:38.109
commissioning control of the business, and
ensuring that they live within their means

00:02:38.109 --> 00:02:44.749
in an area which is characterised by growing
demands and, indeed, volatility of demand.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.120
We have high levels of confidence in that
forecast, mainly because some of the cost

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:54.409
risks that we anticipated in central specialised
commissioning budgets are not yet materialising

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:55.849
to the extent expected.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.640
There are small underspends, as you can see,
in the other direct commissioning budgets,

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:05.159
but the big action is in the central budgets,
where we are continuing to seek reductions

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:10.459
and savings as an offset to the challenges
in the CCG sector. We have increased that

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:15.489
by £80 million to a total of £363 million
since we last met, and we are continuing to

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:21.209
freeze underspends as they occur. The oddities
of Treasury accounting are reflected in the

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:25.659
adjustments towards the bottom of the table.
These have improved slightly since month seven,

00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:30.900
as CCGs in particular have refined their estimates
of provision movements, leaving us with a

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.519
bottom line of a £5 million underspend, which,
you might like to know, is equivalent roughly

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:41.999
to 25 minutes of NHS expenditure, just to
give you a sense of the relevance of a £5

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.499
million landing strip.

00:03:43.499 --> 00:03:50.150
I have set out at paragraphs 12 and 13 our
risk assessment against that position, largely

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:55.209
consisting of the CCG risks outlined earlier,
most of which, I have to say, seem likely

00:03:55.209 --> 00:04:00.069
to materialise, so our success in meeting
our financial objectives for the year depends

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:05.599
largely on our ability to find additional
mitigations elsewhere. Either way, we will

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:09.341
deliver a substantial contribution to financial
balance across the health sector when the

00:04:09.341 --> 00:04:14.669
£800 million risk reserve is brought into
play. In that context, I do have to say that,

00:04:14.669 --> 00:04:20.199
as I indicated in December, it does look very
likely, if not certain, that the £800 million

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:24.830
reserve regrettably will need to be released
to commissioners’ bottom lines to secure

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:29.810
overall financial balance, rather than being
available for investment.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:34.710
I commend the report to the Board, and, as always, look forward to your questions and suggestions.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.400
Noel Gordon: It is just worth recording that

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.820
we in the Commissioning Committee when through
this in great detail yesterday.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.879
Malcolm Grant: Not all the Board are on the
Commissioning Committee, but those who are

00:04:53.879 --> 00:04:58.780
on it had a very good presentation and a very
good discussion yesterday, so on that basis,

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:04.509
Paul, I think we can excuse you from further
service this morning.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.639
Simon Stevens: Just one thing that we drew out
of the Commissioning Committee, given that

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:18.550
Paul was not there for that part of the conversation.
Despite the £100 million-plus risk that is

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:23.940
still there, we think we are on track for
a balanced budget, with £800 million of managed

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:31.229
underspend to provide cover for the trust
position, as Paul said, but that £800 million

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:40.780
is obviously dependent on CCGs not using any
of their 1% or increasing their overspend

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.009
in support of their local providers. To the
extent that they do that, then that is money

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:49.150
that is coming off the £800 million which
is needed for the provider sector nationally,

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:56.080
so it is not a sensible thing to do, given
that we need that £800 million to help the

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.780
trust position nationally, and I think that
is just something that we need to make sure

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.020
is understood right through the system.

